<Prose
  title="About Howloop"
  description="Why Howloop exists, who makes it, and how the animated guides are put together."
  lede="Howloop turns ordinary how-to instructions into short looping animations, because most of the confusing part of a repair is the part you can't see."
>
  <h2>The problem we kept running into</h2>
  <p>
    A slow bathroom drain is not a hard problem. Neither is a toilet that runs
    all night, or a phone that says it has no storage left. They are all
    problems where the fix takes ten minutes and the <em>understanding</em> takes
    an hour — because the thing you need to see is hidden inside a pipe, inside
    a tank, or inside a menu six levels deep.
  </p>
  <p>
    So you search. You get a wall of text with no pictures, or a fourteen-minute
    video where the actual fix starts at 8:40, or a photo of someone's hand
    holding a tool at an angle that tells you nothing. None of it shows you the
    one thing you needed: what is happening on the other side of the wall.
  </p>

  <h2>What we do instead</h2>
  <p>
    Every guide on {SITE.name} is built around a short animation that cuts the
    object open. You see the clog sitting in the tailpiece. You see the flapper
    not quite sealing. You see where the paper template goes before the nail
    does. Then you get the same instructions in writing, in the same order, so
    you can print them or read them one-handed while the other hand is busy.
  </p>
  <p>The animations are deliberately plain:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Every step is a state you can stop on.</strong> Nothing loops past
      you at a speed set by someone else. Pause, go back, sit on step three for
      as long as you need.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Colour means something.</strong> Green is the correct path and the
      direction things should move. Orange is the part you actually touch.
      Everything else is context.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>They are drawings, not footage.</strong> A drawing can remove the
      cabinet door, the wall and the plastic housing. A camera can't.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How a guide gets made</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      We pick a problem that recurs — something a normal household hits more than
      once, not a one-off exotic repair.
    </li>
    <li>
      We work out the actual mechanism: <em>why</em> the thing fails, not just
      what to do about it. If we can't explain the cause in one sentence, the
      guide isn't ready.
    </li>
    <li>
      We storyboard the fix as four to six states. If it needs more than six,
      the guide is really two guides.
    </li>
    <li>
      We draw it, write the steps to match exactly, and add the honest caveats:
      when this won't work, and when to stop and call someone.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Full detail on sourcing, corrections and review is in our
    <a href="/editorial-policy">editorial policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What we won't do</h2>
  <p>
    We don't publish guides for anything where a mistake is dangerous and
    invisible — gas work, mains electrical work, structural work, or anything
    medical beyond ordinary first aid. When a job crosses that line, the guide
    says so and stops. A page view is not worth a house fire.
  </p>

  <h2>Who's behind it</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} is a small independent site. It is funded by advertising, which
    is why you'll see clearly labelled ad slots between sections. Ads never
    influence which guides we make or what they say — see our
    <a href="/editorial-policy">editorial policy</a> for how we keep that line
    clean.
  </p>
  <p>
    Found a mistake, or fixed something in a smarter way than we showed? We'd
    honestly like to know: <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>{SITE.email}</a>.
  </p>
</Prose>
